# Redstone 9X — declarative desktop icon layout.
# One .desktop basename per line, in display order, column-major (down the
# first column, then across).  Apply with: loom redstone:layout
#
#   "---"            on its own line starts a new column
#   "name @anchor"   pins absolutely and takes no part in the column flow;
#                    anchors are bottom-right, bottom-left, top-right,
#                    top-left, or an explicit "@X,Y" in screen pixels
#
# Blank lines and # comments are ignored.  Names not present in ~/Desktop are
# skipped, and icons not named here auto-arrange around the pinned ones.
#
# System icons lead, in the Windows 95 shell's own order, then shells and DOS,
# then everything else alphabetically by display name.  No explicit column
# breaks: the list simply flows, which is what a real Win9x desktop does.
#
# NOTE: Network Neighborhood belongs at position 2 but has no launcher yet.
# Adding one and inserting it here shifts the rest down by one automatically.
my-computer.desktop
program-manager.desktop
inbox.desktop
my-documents.desktop
my-briefcase.desktop
aol-online.desktop
firefox.desktop
msn-online.desktop
dosemu2.desktop
dosbox-x.desktop
cmd.desktop
powershell.desktop
bash-cmd.desktop
zsh-ps.desktop
command-conquer.desktop
red-alert.desktop
diablo.desktop
hellfire.desktop
duke-nukem-3d.desktop
half-life.desktop
blue-shift.desktop
opposing-force.desktop
mupen64plus.desktop
quake.desktop
snes9x.desktop
theme-hospital.desktop
ultimate-doom.desktop
zsnes.desktop
# Bottom-right corner, clear of the build stamp — the Win95 shell's own resting
# place for the bin. The anchor tracks the build stamp's real geometry, so it
# stays correct if its fonts or margins change.
recycle-bin.desktop @bottom-right
